


## 0. Create a User with adduser

### Basic user creation (Interactive)
```bash
# Create user with interactive prompts
sudo adduser john

# This will automatically:
# - Create home directory
# - Set up default shell (/bin/bash)
# - Prompt for password
# - Ask for user details (name, phone, etc.)
# - Create a group with same name as user
```

### Create user with specific options
```bash
# Create user with custom shell
sudo useradd john --shell /bin/sh

# Create user with custom home directory
sudo useradd john --home /opt/users/john

# Create user without creating home directory
sudo useradd john --no-create-home

# Create system user (for services)
sudo useradd --system serviceuser

# Create user with specific UID
sudo useradd john --uid 1500

# Create user with specific GID
sudo useradd john --gid 1000
```

### Create user with disabled login options
```bash
# Create user with disabled login (cannot login)
sudo useradd john --disabled-login

# Create user without password (can login with SSH keys)
sudo useradd john --disabled-password

# Create user in specific group
sudo useradd john --ingroup developers
```

## User Management Files

These files are essential for managing users, groups, and permissions on a Linux system, and they play a key role in ensuring security and efficient system administration.

![[6b0ab3395759977ed35163f12cb36df8_MD5.webp]]

The following are different user management files in linux:

###### User Information

**`**/etc/passwd:**`** Stores basic details of all user accounts including:

- Username
- User ID (UID)
- Primary Group ID (GID)
- Home directory
- Default shell
- Full name of the user
