   ## 1. File Permissions and Ownership

   ### Understanding Permissions
   Linux uses a permission system with three types of access:
   - **Read (r)**: View file contents or list directory
   - **Write (w)**: Modify file or add/remove files in directory
   - **Execute (x)**: Run file as program or enter directory

   ### Permission Groups
   - **Owner (u)**: File owner
   - **Group (g)**: Users in the file's group
   - **Others (o)**: Everyone else

   ### Viewing Permissions
   ```bash
   # Detailed file listing
   ls -l filename.txt
   # Output: -rw-r--r-- 1 user group 1024 Jan 15 10:30 filename.txt
   #         ^^^^^^^^^ ^   ^    ^     ^   ^  ^  ^  ^  ^
   #         |         |   |    |     |   |  |  |  |  |
   #         |         |   |    |     |   |  |  |  |  +-- File name
   #         |         |   |    |     |   |  |  |  +----- Time
   #         |         |   |    |     |   |  |  +-------- Date
   #         |         |   |    |     |   |  +----------- Size
   #         |         |   |    |     |   +-------------- Group
   #         |         |   |    |     +------------------ Owner
   #         |         |   |    +------------------------ Links
   #         |         |   +----------------------------- Group permissions
   #         |         +---------------------------------- Owner permissions
   #         +-------------------------------------------- File type & permissions
   ```

   ### Changing Permissions
   ```bash
   # Using chmod with symbolic notation
   chmod u+x filename.txt        # Add execute for owner
   chmod g-w filename.txt        # Remove write for group
   chmod o+r filename.txt        # Add read for others
   chmod a+x filename.txt        # Add execute for all (a = all)
   sudo chmod -x devolopers/

   # Using chmod with octal notation
   chmod 755 filename.txt        # rwxr-xr-x
   chmod 644 filename.txt        # rw-r--r--
   chmod 600 filename.txt        # rw-------

   •	7 (Owner): read (4) + write (2) + execute (1) = rwx (full access)
   •	7 (Group): read (4) + write (2) + execute (1) = rwx (full access)
   •	5 (Others): read (4) + execute (1) = r-x (read and execute only)
   ```



   ### Changing Ownership
   ```bash
   # Change owner
   chown newowner filename.txt

   # Change owner and group
   chown newowner:newgroup filename.txt

   # Change only group
   chgrp newgroup filename.txt

   # Recursive change
   chown -R user:group directory/
   ```



   ## 2. Change User Password

   ```bash
   # Set password for user (will prompt for password)
   sudo passwd john

   # Change your own password
   passwd

   # Force password change at next login
   sudo passwd -e john

   # Set password expiry
   sudo chage -M 90 john  # Password expires in 90 days

   # Set minimum password age
   sudo chage -m 7 john   # Must wait 7 days before changing

   # Set account expiry date
   sudo chage -E 2025-12-31 john
   ```

   ## 3. Create and Manage Groups

   ### Create groups using adduser
   ```bash
   # Create a group
   sudo addgroup developers

   sudo adduser --group developers

   # Create system group
   sudo adduser --system --group systemservice
   ```

   ### Add user to groups
   ```bash
   # Add user to supplementary group (append)
   usermod -aG groupname username
   ```

   ### View user groups
   ```bash
   # Show user's groups
   groups john
   id john

   # Show all groups
   getent group

   # Show members of a group
   getent group developers
   ```

   ## 4. Remove User from Group

   ```bash
   # Remove user from specific group
   sudo deluser john developers

   # Remove user from all supplementary groups
   sudo usermod -G "" john
   ```

   ## 5. Delete Groups

   ```bash
   # Delete a group
   sudo delgroup developers

   # Alternative command
   sudo groupdel developers

   # Check if group exists before deleting
   getent group developers && sudo delgroup developers
   ```

   ## 6. File and Directory Permissions

   ### Change ownership
   ```bash
   # Change owner
   sudo chown john /home/project

   # Change owner and group
   sudo chown john:developers /home/project

   # Recursive change
   sudo chown -R john:developers /home/project/
   ```

   ### Change permissions
   ```bash
   # Numeric permissions
   sudo chmod 755 /home/project          # rwxr-xr-x
   sudo chmod 644 /home/project/file.txt  # rw-r--r--

   # Symbolic permissions
   sudo chmod u+rwx,g+rx,o+rx /home/project
   sudo chmod g+w /home/project
   sudo chmod o-rwx /home/project

   # Set setgid bit (files inherit group)
   sudo chmod 2775 /home/shared_project
   ```




   ### SUID (Set User ID) - 4

   **What it does**: File runs with **owner's permissions**, not yours.

   **Example**:
   ```bash
   # The passwd command runs as root so you can change your password
   ls -l /usr/bin/passwd
   -rwsr-xr-x 1 root root 68208 passwd

   # Set SUID
   chmod 4755 file
   chmod u+s file
   ```

   **Real use**: When you run `passwd`, it needs root access to modify `/etc/shadow`, even though you're a regular user[1][2].

   ***

   ### SGID (Set Group ID) - 2

   **What it does**: 
   - **Files**: Run with group's permissions
   - **Directories**: New files inherit the directory's group

   **Example**:
   ```bash
   # Directory with SGID - perfect for team folders
   chmod 2775 /shared/project
   chmod g+s /shared/project

   # Result: drwxrwsr-x
   # Now any file created inside gets the "project" group automatically
   ```
   [[665c97449517bb6dc562545ddc076846_MD5.jpg|Open: Pasted image 20251010012342.png]]
   ![[665c97449517bb6dc562545ddc076846_MD5.jpg]]
   **Real use**: Team collaboration - everyone's files belong to the same group[3][4].

   ***

   ### Sticky Bit - 1

   **What it does**: In directories, only **file owners can delete their own files**.

   **Example**:
   ```bash
   # /tmp directory uses sticky bit
   ls -ld /tmp
   drwxrwxrwt 15 root root 4096 /tmp

   # Set sticky bit
   chmod 1777 directory
   chmod +t directory

   sudo useradd user1
   sudo useradd user2

   sudo su - user2
   cd /shared_lab
   rm user1_file.txt    # This SUCCEEDS without sticky bit
   ls -l
   exit

   sudo chmod +t /shared_lab

   sudo su - user1
   cd /shared_lab
   touch protected_file.txt
   echo "Protected content" > protected_file.txt
   ls -l
   exit

   ```


   | Permission | Number | Symbol        | Effect                  |
   | ---------- | ------ | ------------- | ----------------------- |
   | SUID       | 4      | `s` in user   | Run as file owner       |
   | SGID       | 2      | `s` in group  | Inherit group ownership |
   | Sticky     | 1      | `t` in others | Only owner can delete   |


